# Envoy view

Envoy view connects to an Envoy server and downloads the xDS configuration.

It then displays all the xDS in a way that is easily consumable for a human by
mapping it into a graph of filter chain pipelines. This will be a Web-based UI
with the nodes being Envoy stages linked with the appropriate xDS configuration.

## Implementation nodes

* Implement in go-lang server that connects to the Envoy server.
* UI runs as a website hosted by the server.

## Demo

If you have a container runtime and just want to see it work:

    make demo

That boots a demo Envoy in a container, starts envoy-view against it, and
opens the UI. Ctrl-C stops both; nothing is left behind. `demo/run.sh
--print` renders the same graph to stdout instead, with no UI.

The demo config in `demo/` is picked to put one of every interesting shape
on the canvas: a weighted route splitting 80/20, an EDS cluster reporting
2 of 3 endpoints healthy, a TCP proxy chain, and one route pointing at a
cluster that does not exist, which is the red node the problems panel
jumps to. Editing `demo/rds.yaml` and hitting Refresh shows a live RDS
update landing in the graph.

## Running it

    go build -o envoy-view ./cmd/envoy-view
    ./envoy-view -envoy-admin 127.0.0.1:9901

Then open http://127.0.0.1:8080. The web UI is compiled into the binary,
so a build needs no npm.

The server fetches `/config_dump` from the Envoy admin interface on demand
and keeps the result as a browsable snapshot; "Refresh" takes a new one.
Nothing is pushed or written back to Envoy — this is read-only.

To look at a dump captured elsewhere, pass it on the command line or drag
the file onto the page:

    curl -s localhost:9901/config_dump?include_eds > dump.json
    ./envoy-view -dump-file dump.json

Useful flags (`-h` for the rest):

| flag | default | |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `-addr` | `127.0.0.1:8080` | where to serve the UI |
| `-envoy-admin` | `127.0.0.1:9901` | Envoy's admin interface |
| `-dump-file` | | read a saved dump instead of querying Envoy |
| `-print` | | render `summary` or `graph` to stdout and exit |

The Envoy admin interface is unauthenticated and exposes secrets and
private keys, so `-addr` binds to localhost by default. Reach a remote
Envoy over an SSH tunnel rather than exposing either port.

## Reading the graph

Pipelines flow left to right: listener → filter chain → filters → route
configuration → virtual host → route → cluster → endpoints. Every node
is a real xDS resource; clicking one opens its config dump entry, with
the version and the time Envoy last updated it.

A red border means the graph builder found a problem — a route pointing
at a cluster that is not in the dump, a rejected update, an EDS cluster
with no endpoints. The problems panel at the bottom lists them all and
jumps to each one. Press `/` to search by name.

## Development

    make ui             # rebuild the embedded UI (needs npm)
    make build          # compile the binary
    make test           # Go tests, then UI tests and typecheck
    make test-short     # skip the container scenarios
    make test-container # boot a real Envoy and test against it
    make ui-dev         # Vite dev server with hot reload, proxying /api

`internal/envoytest` runs the pinned Envoy release in a container and feeds
its real config dump to the parser, so the fixtures are transcripts rather
than guesses. Those tests skip when no container runtime is present.
See `docs/testing-plan.md`.

`internal/webui/dist` is committed, so `make ui` is only needed after
changing anything under `web/`. Because `go:embed` bakes it in at compile
time, always rebuild the binary after rebuilding the UI.
